Sure! Here’s a quick way to add product photos to the site and have them show up both on the product‑listing page and inside the cart.

---

**1. Create a folder for your images**  
In your Flask project create a folder called `static/images`.  
Put each product’s picture in that folder (e.g., `product‑1.jpg`, `product‑2.jpg`, …).

**2. Make the product data include an image URL**  
If you keep the product list in `script.js` as an array of objects, add an `image` field:

```javascript
const products = [
  {
    id: 1,
    name: "Cool T‑Shirt",
    price: 19.99,
    image: "/static/images/product-1.jpg"
  },
  {
    id: 2,
    name: "Stylish Mug",
    price: 12.5,
    image: "/static/images/product-2.jpg"
  }
  // …more products
];
```

**3. Show the image on the product page (`product.html`)**  
Inside the loop that creates each product card, insert an `<img>` tag that uses the `image` property:

```html
<div class="product-card" data-id="{{product.id}}">
  <img src="{{product.image}}" alt="{{product.name}}" class="product-photo" style="max-width:100%;height:auto;">
  <h3>{{product.name}}</h3>
  <p>$ {{product.price}}</p>
  <button class="add-to-cart">Add to Cart</button>
</div>
```

If you’re generating the cards with JavaScript, update the `renderProducts` function in `script.js` to include the same `<img>` markup.

**4. Show the image in the cart (`cart.html` / `script.js`)**  
In the `renderCart` function, when you build each row, add an image cell:

```javascript
cartItems.forEach(item => {
  const row = document.createElement('tr');

  const imgCell = document.createElement('td');
  const img = document.createElement('img');
  img.src = item.image;
  img.alt = item.name;
  img.style.width = "60px";
  img.style.height = "auto";
  imgCell.appendChild(img);
  row.appendChild(imgCell);

  const nameCell = document.createElement('td');
  nameCell.textContent = item.name;
  row.appendChild(nameCell);

  const qtyCell = document.createElement('td');
  qtyCell.textContent = item.quantity;
  row.appendChild(qtyCell);

  const priceCell = document.createElement('td');
  priceCell.textContent = `$ ${item.price.toFixed(2)}`;
  row.appendChild(priceCell);

  const totalCell = document.createElement('td');
  totalCell.textContent = `$ ${(item.price * item.quantity).toFixed(2)}`;
  row.appendChild(totalCell);

  cartTableBody.appendChild(row);
});
```

Add a corresponding `<th>` for the image column in `cart.html`:

```html
<table id="cart-table">
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Photo</th>
      <th>Product</th>
      <th>Qty</th>
      <th>Price</th>
      <th>Total</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody id="cart-items"></tbody>
</table>
```

**5. Ensure Flask can serve the static images**  
Your existing `app.py` already serves the `static` folder by default, so no extra code is needed. Just make sure the image paths start with `/static/…` as shown above.

**6. (Optional) Use placeholder images while you gather the real ones**  
If you don’t have the final pictures yet, you can use a free placeholder service:

```javascript
image: "https://via.placeholder.com/300x200?text=Cool+T-Shirt"
```

Replace the placeholder URL with your real `/static/images/…` path when the final photos are ready.

---

### Quick checklist

- [ ] Add `static/images` directory.
- [ ] Drop each product photo into that folder.
- [ ] Extend the product objects with an `image` field (use relative path `/static/images/…`).
- [ ] Update the product‑card HTML (or the JS `renderProducts` function) to include an `<img>` tag.
- [ ] Add an image column header in `cart.html`.
- [ ] Modify `renderCart` in `script.js` to render the image cell.
- [ ] Refresh the page – you should now see photos next to every product and inside the cart.

Let me know if you run into any trouble or if you’d like help wiring the image URLs into a database instead of a hard‑coded array. Happy coding!
